B-0 (Boosty) audit r1 F4 -- stdio over a socket: the FILE backends honour the
pouch socket fd.

A pouch socket fd is not a kernel handle. socket() -- and accept() on an
AF_INET listener -- return POUCH_SOCK_TAG | slot (0006, 0016); read(), write(),
close(), poll() and the socket calls map the tag to the slot's kernel handle
before they trap. (An AF_UNIX accept() returns a plain kernel fd, so the
server end of a /srv stream never had this problem; every client end did.) The
four stdio backends never did: __stdio_read / __stdio_write / __stdio_close
handed f->fd to the kernel raw, and the kernel has never issued 0x4000000N.

So since 0006:
  - fdopen(sock, ...) returned a FILE on which every read and write failed
    (EBADF -> F_ERR), and dprintf(sock, ...) wrote nothing;
  - fclose() on it "closed" a number the kernel did not know and STRANDED the
    slot with the kernel handles it holds. The table is POUCH_SOCK_MAX = 8
    wide, so eight such fclose()s ended socket() for the life of the Proc.

Pre-existing; found by the audit of 0035, which rewrote __stdio_read and
preserved the defect. No program under usr/ and nothing in stratumd calls
fdopen() at all (grepped 2026-09-21), which is how it survived: the first port
that wraps a connected socket in a FILE would have hit it.

The fix is the mapping read()/write()/close() already use, in the three
backends, plus the POSIX answer for a seek:
  - read / write: pouch_sock_kernel_fd(f->fd) -- identity for an untagged fd; a
    slot with no handle yet (socket() without connect()) is F_ERR with the
    map's errno (ENOTCONN / EBADF), as read() reports it.
  - close: a tagged fd goes through pouch_sock_close(), which frees the slot
    and closes its data, ctl and ready handles.
  - seek: ESPIPE for a tagged fd, instead of the kernel's EBADF for a number it
    never issued. ftell()/fseek()/rewind() on a socket stream now fail the way
    POSIX says they do.

Cost, stated: the backends now reference _pouch_socket.o (3.8 KB, no further
dependencies) from every program that links stdio. read.c / write.c / close.c
already pull it into nearly all of them.

NOT covered, recorded: freopen() onto a socket stream dup3()s onto the raw tag
and fails cleanly (the stream is closed, as freopen does on any failure);
__fdopen's F_SETFD / F_SETFL for the "e" and "a" modes are raw fcntl calls
whose results upstream already ignores. Neither fabricates a value.

Device-side pin (same commit, not in this patch file): usr/pouch-hello/
pouch-hello-sockets.c fdopen()s the connected client end, writes a line through
the FILE, reads the reply with fscanf + fgets (0035's pushback path, over a
socket), requires fseek -> ESPIPE, fclose()s it, and then requires the number
of free socket slots to equal the number measured before the test -- measured
by opening sockets until refusal, not mirrored from POUCH_SOCK_MAX.

--- a/src/stdio/__stdio_close.c
+++ b/src/stdio/__stdio_close.c
@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@
 #include "stdio_impl.h"
 #include "aio_impl.h"
+#include "_pouch_socket.h"
 
 static int dummy(int fd)
 {
@@ -10,5 +11,10 @@
 
 int __stdio_close(FILE *f)
 {
+	/* pouch: a socket fd is a libc-side slot. Closing it raw would hand the
+	 * kernel a number it never issued (EBADF) and strand the slot and the
+	 * handles it holds -- the table is small, so a few fclose()s of socket
+	 * streams would end socket() for the whole Proc. */
+	if (f->fd & POUCH_SOCK_TAG) return pouch_sock_close(f->fd);
 	return syscall(SYS_close, __aio_close(f->fd));
 }
